      <h1 id="core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="group scroll-mt-20">Prepare {"{"}{"{"} rh_rhacm {"}"}{"}"} policies and {"{"}{"{"} cgu_operator {"}"}{"}"} for cluster updates<a href="#core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Before you can perform policy-based cluster updates, you must configure your hub cluster with the required Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) policies, placement rules, and Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager (TALM) <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> custom resources (CRs).</p>
      <h2 id="core-cluster-upgrade-talm-setup_core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="group scroll-mt-20">Preparing RHACM policies and placement rules for cluster updates<a href="#core-cluster-upgrade-talm-setup_core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can organize your Git repository, configure cluster labels, and create placement rules to target clusters for policy-based updates.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>RHACM hub cluster is deployed and managing target clusters. For more information, see <a href="https://access.redhat.com/documentation/en-us/red_hat_advanced_cluster_management_for_kubernetes/">Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management for Kubernetes documentation</a>.</li>
      <li>Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager (TALM) is installed on the RHACM hub cluster. For more information, see "Installing Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager by using the CLI".</li>
      <li>You have a Git repository for storing update policies with appropriate access controls.</li>
      <li>You have cluster-admin privileges on the RHACM hub cluster.</li>
      <li>The <code>oc</code> CLI tool is installed and configured.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Prepare your Git repository structure similar to the following example:
      <CodeBlock code={"upgrade-policies/\n├── policies/\n│   ├── zstream/\n│   │   └── upgrade-4.20.1-policy.yaml\n│   ├── ystream/\n│   │   └── upgrade-4.20-policy.yaml\n│   └── eus/\n│       └── upgrade-4.20-eus-policy.yaml\n└── cgu/\n    ├── core-zstream-upgrade.yaml\n    ├── core-ystream-upgrade.yaml\n    └── core-eus-upgrade.yaml"} lang="text" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If you are using Kustomize for policy management with Argo CD or Flux, you can add a <code>kustomization.yaml</code> file to manage policy overlays. This file is optional if you apply policies directly with <code>oc apply</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Placement</code> rule so that update policies can be bound to clusters through labels.
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If you use PolicyGenerator with Argo CD or a ZTP pipeline, <code>Placement</code> and <code>PlacementBinding</code> resources are generated automatically from your PolicyGenerator configuration. The following manual steps are provided for environments that do not use PolicyGenerator or for reference when troubleshooting placement issues.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p>The following example uses the label <code>upgrade-version-to-4-21=""</code> to bind a policy to a cluster:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1beta1\nkind: Placement\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-upgrade-placement\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\n  annotations:\n    policy.open-cluster-management.io/description: |\n      Placement rule for OpenShift upgrade policies. Targets clusters with\n      the label \"upgrade-version-to-4-21\" for 4.20 to 4.21 upgrades.\nspec:\n  predicates:\n    - requiredClusterSelector:\n        labelSelector:\n          matchLabels:\n            upgrade-version-to-4-21: \"\""} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>PlacementBinding</code> resource to connect the policies to the placement rule:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: PlacementBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-upgrade-placement-binding\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\n  annotations:\n    policy.open-cluster-management.io/description: |\n      Binding that connects OpenShift upgrade policies to their placement rule.\n      This binding ensures that both the main upgrade policy and pre-upgrade\n      health check policy are applied to the same set of target clusters.\nplacementRef:\n  name: openshift-upgrade-placement\n  kind: Placement\n  apiGroup: cluster.open-cluster-management.io\nsubjects:\n  - name: openshift-upgrade-4.21-policy\n    kind: Policy\n    apiGroup: policy.open-cluster-management.io\n  - name: pre-upgrade-health-check-policy\n    kind: Policy\n    apiGroup: policy.open-cluster-management.io"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>A single policy, placement rule, and placement binding set can be bound to multiple clusters by adding the matching label to each cluster.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Add an update label to your target clusters and verify by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc label managedcluster <cluster_name> upgrade-version-to-4-21=\"\"\n$ oc get managedcluster <cluster_name> -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n  \"app.kubernetes.io/instance\": \"clusters\",\n  \"cluster.open-cluster-management.io/clusterset\": \"default\",\n  \"name\": \"test1\",\n  \"ocp-version\": \"4.20\",\n  \"openshiftVersion\": \"4.20.15\",\n  \"upgrade-version-to-4-21\": \"\",\n  \"upgrade-version-to-4-22\": \"\",\n  \"unpause-worker-mcp\": \"\"\n}"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: Label clusters by site location for topology awareness by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc label managedcluster <cluster_name> site=<site_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create and apply a <code>ManagedClusterSet</code> resource for update targets:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1beta2\nkind: ManagedClusterSet\nmetadata:\n  name: core-bm-clusters\nspec:\n  clusterSelector:\n    selectorType: LabelSelector\n    labelSelector:\n      matchLabels:\n        cluster-type: bare-metal-core"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Apply the <code>ManagedClusterSet</code> resource by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f managedclusterset.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify that TALM is active, cluster labels are applied, and the <code>ManagedClusterSet</code> resource exists by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-operators | grep cluster-group-upgrades\n$ oc get managedclusters --show-labels\n$ oc get managedclusterset core-bm-clusters"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"cluster-group-upgrades-controller-manager-5d7b9c8f7d-abc12   2/2     Running   0          5m"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="core-cluster-upgrade-cgu-configuration_core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="group scroll-mt-20">ClusterGroupUpgrade custom resource configuration<a href="#core-cluster-upgrade-cgu-configuration_core-cluster-upgrades-preparing-policies" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can configure <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> custom resources (CRs) to orchestrate cluster updates with Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager (TALM). A <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR defines which clusters to update, which policies to apply, and how to manage batching and concurrency.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>RHACM policies used for updates must be set to <code>inform</code> mode. The <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR temporarily changes these policies to <code>enforce</code> mode at the scheduled update time, applying them to the target clusters. When the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR completes or times out, the policies automatically revert to <code>inform</code> mode. This ensures that update policies are only enforced at the specific time you intend, rather than immediately when the policy is created.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h3 id="_basic_clustergroupupgrade_cr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Basic ClusterGroupUpgrade CR<a href="#_basic_clustergroupupgrade_cr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>The following example shows a basic <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: <cgu_name>\n  namespace: <namespace>\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - <cluster_name_1>\n  - <cluster_name_2>\n  managedPolicies:\n  - <policy_name>\n  enable: false\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: <max_concurrency>\n    timeout: <timeout_minutes>"} lang="yaml" />
      <p><strong>ClusterGroupUpgrade CR fields</strong></p>
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>Field</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Description</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>name</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies a name for your <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR, for example <code>core-zstream-upgrade-4.20.1</code>.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>namespace</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies the namespace for the resource, for example <code>openshift-upgrade-policies</code>.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>clusters</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies the names of your target clusters.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>managedPolicies</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies the names of your update policies.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>enable</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Set to <code>false</code> to create the resource without starting the update. Set to <code>true</code> to start the update immediately.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>maxConcurrency</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies the number of clusters to update simultaneously. Use <code>1</code> for canary testing, <code>2-5</code> for production rollouts, or higher for large fleets with proven procedures.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>timeout</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Specifies the maximum time in minutes to wait for each cluster update. See the timeout guidelines that follow.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <h3 id="_timeout_guidelines" class="group scroll-mt-20">Timeout guidelines<a href="#_timeout_guidelines" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>Calculate appropriate timeout values based on your environment:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Base timeout: 60 minutes for z-stream updates, 120 minutes for y-stream updates.</li>
      <li>Add 30 minutes for each additional worker node beyond 3.</li>
      <li>Add 60 minutes for disconnected environments.</li>
      <li>Add 30 minutes for clusters with large image registries.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="_optional_configuration_fields" class="group scroll-mt-20">Optional configuration fields<a href="#_optional_configuration_fields" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>You can add the following optional fields to the <code>spec</code> section to customize the update behavior:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Canary cluster testing</li>
      <li>Pre-update blocking checks</li>
      <li>Post-update label management</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="_canary_cluster_testing" class="group scroll-mt-20">Canary cluster testing<a href="#_canary_cluster_testing" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>To test the update on a canary cluster before rolling it out to the full fleet, add a <code>canaries</code> list under <code>remediationStrategy</code> and use <code>clusterSelector</code> instead of <code>clusters</code> to target clusters by label:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"  clusterSelector:\n  - <label_selector>\n  remediationStrategy:\n    canaries:\n    - <canary_cluster_name>\n    maxConcurrency: 1"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;label_selector&gt;</code>: Specifies a label selector for the target clusters, for example <code>upgrade-wave=1</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;canary_cluster_name&gt;</code>: Specifies the name of the canary cluster to update first. TALM updates the canary cluster first, waits for success, and then proceeds with other clusters.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="_pre-update_blocking_checks" class="group scroll-mt-20">Pre-update blocking checks<a href="#_pre-update_blocking_checks" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>To require pre-update checks to pass before the update begins, add <code>blockingCRs</code> and <code>beforeEnable</code> fields:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"  beforeEnable:\n    addClusterLabels:\n      pre-upgrade-check: pending\n  blockingCRs:\n  - name: <blocking_cr_name>\n    namespace: <blocking_cr_namespace>\n    kind: ConfigMap\n    conditions:\n    - type: <condition_type>\n      status: \"True\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;blocking_cr_name&gt;</code>: Specifies the name of the blocking custom resource, for example <code>etcd-backup</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;condition_type&gt;</code>: Specifies the condition that must be met before the update can proceed, for example <code>BackupComplete</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="_post-update_label_management" class="group scroll-mt-20">Post-update label management<a href="#_post-update_label_management" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>To update cluster labels after the update completes, add an <code>afterCompletion</code> field:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"  afterCompletion:\n    addClusterLabels:\n      upgraded-to: \"<target_version>\"\n      upgrade-status: complete\n    removeClusterLabels:\n    - pre-upgrade-check"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where <code>&lt;target_version&gt;</code> is the target version, for example <code>4.20</code>.</p>
      <h3 id="_chained_clustergroupupgrade_crs_for_eus-to-eus_updates" class="group scroll-mt-20">Chained ClusterGroupUpgrade CRs for EUS-to-EUS updates<a href="#_chained_clustergroupupgrade_crs_for_eus-to-eus_updates" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>For Extended Update Support (EUS) to EUS updates, you can create chained <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CRs that use <code>blockingCRs</code> and <code>actions</code> to orchestrate a multi-step update:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"---\napiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: upgrade-20to21\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  actions:\n    afterCompletion:\n      deleteClusterLabels:\n        upgrade-version-to-4-21: \"\"\n      deleteObjects: true\n      addClusterLabels:\n        unpause-workers-4-22: \"\"\n  backup: false\n  clusters:\n  - <cluster_name>\n  enable: true\n  managedPolicies:\n    - prep-20to22\n    - upgrade-ocp-20to21\n    - upgrade-olm-20to21\n    - upgrade-validate-21\n  preCaching: false\n  preCachingConfigRef: {}\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1\n    timeout: 480\n---\napiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: upgrade-21to22\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  actions:\n    afterCompletion:\n      deleteClusterLabels:\n        upgrade-version-4-22: \"\"\n        unpause-workers-4-22: \"\"\n      deleteObjects: true\n  backup: false\n  clusters:\n  - <cluster_name>\n  enable: true\n  managedPolicies:\n    - upgrade-ocp-21to22\n    - upgrade-olm-21to22\n    - upgrade-validate-22\n    - upgrade-worker-1\n    - upgrade-worker-2\n  preCaching: false\n  preCachingConfigRef: {}\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1\n    timeout: 480\n  blockingCRs:\n  - name: upgrade-20to21\n    namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;cluster_name&gt;</code>: Specifies the name of the target cluster.</li>
      <li><code>actions</code>: Changes cluster labels as each step completes. This removes temporary update labels and unbinds completed update policies from the cluster.</li>
      <li><code>blockingCRs</code>: Requires the first <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR to complete before the second <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR begins enforcing its policies.</li>
      <li><code>upgrade-worker-1</code>, <code>upgrade-worker-2</code>: Separate unpause policies for each <code>MachineConfigPool</code> resource. Your cluster might have more machine config pools, requiring additional policies.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="_applying_and_monitoring_a_clustergroupupgrade_cr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Applying and monitoring a ClusterGroupUpgrade CR<a href="#_applying_and_monitoring_a_clustergroupupgrade_cr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>After creating a <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR, apply and enable it by running the following commands:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <cgu_cr_filename>.yaml\n$ oc patch cgu <cgu_name> \\\n  -n <namespace> \\\n  --type merge \\\n  -p '{\"spec\":{\"enable\":true}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Monitor the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> status by running the following commands:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu <cgu_name> -n <namespace> -o yaml\n$ oc get cgu <cgu_name> -n <namespace> -w"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Check the <code>status</code> section for update progress:</p>
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"status:\n  conditions:\n  - message: Upgrade is progressing\n    reason: InProgress\n    status: \"True\"\n    type: Progressing\n  managedPoliciesForUpgrade:\n  - name: core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy\n    namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\n  remediationPlan:\n  - - spoke1\n    - spoke2\n  status:\n    spoke1: complete\n    spoke2: inprogress"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Verify the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR entered the expected state by running the following commands:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu -n <namespace>\n$ oc get cgu <cgu_name> -n <namespace> -o jsonpath='{.status.clusters}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                             AGE   STATE        DETAILS\ncore-zstream-upgrade-4.20.1     5m    InProgress   Remediating non-compliant policies"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>When the cluster is fully updated and the state of all <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CRs shows <code>Completed</code>, verify that all policies show <code>Compliant</code> by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get policies -n <cluster_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                        REMEDIATION ACTION   COMPLIANCE STATE   AGE\nupgrade.upgrade-ocp-21to22  inform               Compliant          1h\nupgrade.upgrade-olm-21to22  inform               Compliant          10m"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Delete the completed <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CRs from the update namespace by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete cgu -n <namespace> <cgu_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <h3 id="_troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting<a href="#_troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <ul>
      <li>If the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR is stuck in <code>Preparing</code> state, verify that all managed policies exist and are bound to target clusters.</li>
      <li>If the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR fails with a timeout error, increase the <code>timeout</code> value in the <code>remediationStrategy</code> section.</li>
      </ul>
